body:

We wrote these tools for our own Zoom F3 recordings. Both are free software,
run locally and remain rough around the edges.

Requires Python 3.11+ and [FFmpeg](https://github.com/FFmpeg/FFmpeg)
(`ffmpeg`, `ffprobe`) on `PATH`. NumPy is installed with the package. Provides
`soundcite` and `windwindow`.

## field-audio-tools

Version 0.1.0 (alpha).

Our first test recording was a three-hour take split across two 32-bit float
mono WAV files. We kept repeating the same two jobs, so we wrote one command
for each.

[Test note 01: one complete run on a long F3 take](/software/demo)

### soundcite

`soundcite` cuts a passage at a given timestamp. Alongside the WAV excerpt, it
writes a listening copy, a spectrogram and a manifest containing source
metadata and checksums.

### windwindow

`windwindow` scans a long take in short windows and measures the low-frequency
energy in each one. We use its CSV output to decide where to listen first. It
also writes a JSON summary and a static report.

<pre class="software-example"><code>soundcite Tr1.WAV Tr2.WAV --start 01:23:40 --duration 20
windwindow Tr1.WAV Tr2.WAV --window 10</code></pre>

The score measures low-frequency dominance, not wind. Water, handling and
vehicles can look similar. Listen before excluding anything.

Both commands run locally with Python 3.11+ and FFmpeg. They accept one mono or
stereo file, or two synchronized mono files. The license is GPL-3.0-or-later.

## Test and availability

The command names and output formats may change while we test them against the
original recordings. We will publish the source after that pass.

[Contact us](/contact) if you have similar F3 files and want to try them.
